{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Behold, a people has gone out from Egypt.</b> 'יָצָא' (has gone out) connotes the past tense, but Balaam said 'הִנֵּה הָעָם הַיּוֹצֵא מִמִּצְרַיִם' — this connotes that now they are going out and traveling. For Balaam came to mention their sin, to say that it is as though they have not yet gone out from Egypt at all, for they are always in evil, and they say, 'נִתְּנָה רֹאשׁ וְנָשׁוּבָה מִצְרָיְמָה' (Numbers 14:4 — let us appoint a leader and return to Egypt), and in every complaint they mention Egypt. And this is proof that their thought is always wandering in Egypt, and separation from Egypt is hard for them, as though they are going out now." "And as for what is written, <b>behold, it has covered the eye of the land</b> — this connotes the past tense, and it is difficult, for are they not this very day covering the eye of the land? And it could be that Balak sent to him to say: this people has sorceries in their hand; therefore I need a man who also has sorceries in his hand. And he brought proof of this, for in Egypt they brought the plague of the locusts, which covered the eye of the land, and the darkness — 'וְהוּא יוֹשֵׁב מִמּוּלִי' (and he dwells opposite me): even though he sits across from me in a single room, he is able to bring darkness upon his enemies while for him there will be light in his dwelling — and this is nothing but the power of sorceries that they learned in Egypt. And Balaam thought that perhaps HaShem would say to him that they have already forgotten all the deeds of the land of Egypt; therefore he changed the wording and said: 'הִנֵּה הָעָם הַיּוֹצֵא מִמִּצְרַיִם' — as though they are going out now. And from the fact that Balaam said in prophecy, 'כִּי לֹא נַחַשׁ בְּיַעֲקֹב וְלֹא קֶסֶם בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל' (Numbers 23:23 — for there is no divination in Jacob, nor sorcery in Israel), hear from this that until now they were suspected of divination and sorcery." "Another interpretation: <b>it has covered the eye of the land.</b> He said this concerning Sihon and Og, who were the eye of all the earth, for the eyes of all the earth were upon them; and since they defeated them, who can stand against them? And so the Yalkut concludes."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:32.921019263Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}